  <static seed="389" source="Library/WHFreeman/Rogawski_Calculus_Early_Transcendentals_Second_Edition/13_Calculus_of_Vector-Valued_Functions/13.2_Calculus_of_Vector-Valued_Functions/13.2.17.pg">
    <statement><p>Use the appropriate Product Rule to evaluate the derivative, where</p>
<p><m>{\bf r}_{1}(t)=\left\lt -9 t,8,-t^{10}\right&gt; ,  
   {\bf r}_{2}(t)=\left\lt -2,e^{t},-3\right&gt;</m></p>
<p><m>\frac{\,d}{\,dt}\left({\bf r}_1(t)\cdot {\bf r}_2(t)\right)=</m><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>8e^{t}+30t^{9}+18</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>\frac{\,d}{\,dt}\bf r\it_1\cdot \bf r\it_2=\mathbf{r}_1\cdot \bf r\it_2'+\bf r\it_1'\cdot \bf r\it_2</m>



We compute the derivatives of <m>\bf r\it_1</m> and <m>\bf r\it_2</m>:



<m>\bf r\it_1'=\frac{\,d}{\,dt}\left\lt -9 t,8,-t^{10}\right&gt;=\left\lt -9,0,-10 t^{9}\right&gt;</m>



<m>\bf r\it_2'=\frac{\,d}{\,dt}\left\lt -2,e^{t},-3\right&gt;=\left\lt 0,e^{t},0\right&gt;</m>



Then,



<m>\frac{\,d}{\,dt}\bf r\it_1(t)\cdot \bf r\it_2(t)=\left\lt -9 t,8,-t^{10}\right&gt;\cdot
  \left\lt 0,e^{t},0\right&gt; + \left\lt -9,0,-10 t^{9}\right&gt;\cdot \left\lt -2,e^{t},-3\right&gt;</m> 


<m>\quad = 8e^{t}+30t^{9}+18</m>


</solution>
